# Overrides merged over the image's /etc/crowdsec/config.yaml. Upstream
# recommends this file rather than replacing config.yaml wholesale, so that we
# inherit their defaults and state only what we are changing.
#
# We change exactly one thing: where the central-API credentials live.
#
# Why this is necessary at all. Enrolment writes a machine identity, and by
# default it writes it to /etc/crowdsec/online_api_credentials.yaml. That
# directory is deliberately NOT a persisted volume, because a volume there would
# shadow the acquisition config, parser and scenario that crowdsec/Dockerfile
# bakes into the image -- and worse, the image only prestages /etc/crowdsec when
# it finds the directory empty, and does so with `rsync --ignore-existing`, so a
# volume would be seeded once at first start and then never refreshed. Editing
# our parser would silently do nothing. So /etc/crowdsec stays ephemeral and the
# credentials move somewhere that persists.
#
# Why this particular path. `/etc/crowdsec/creds` does not exist in the image, so
# mounting a volume there shadows nothing at all. It is a separate volume from
# the decisions database, deliberately: wiping decisions to start the buckets
# afresh should not also throw away our registration with CrowdSec.
#
# How this interacts with the image's startup script, which is the subtle part.
# /docker_start.sh registers with the central API only when this key is *absent*:
#
#     config_exists=$(conf_get '.api.server.online_client | has("credentials_path")')
#     if isfalse "$config_exists"; then
#         ... cscli capi register > "$CONFIG_DIR/online_api_credentials.yaml"
#     fi
#
# and `conf_get` reads the *merged* configuration via `cscli config show-yaml`,
# so it sees this file. Setting the key therefore switches automatic registration
# off for good. That is what we want -- otherwise, with /etc/crowdsec ephemeral,
# every deploy would register a brand-new machine upstream and leave a trail of
# junk registrations -- but it means **the first registration has to be done by
# hand, once**:
#
#     just cscli capi register > /etc/crowdsec/creds/online_api_credentials.yaml
#
# See "Phase 5b" in docs/perf/crowdsec-plan.md, which records how that was run and
# how to tell whether it worked. Until it is run, CrowdSec has the central API
# enabled but no credentials, so it quietly neither reports nor receives anything.
api:
  server:
    online_client:
      credentials_path: /etc/crowdsec/creds/online_api_credentials.yaml
